EGO-SELECT: The Publishable Result We Are Hoping For
=====================================================
2026-07-11

Written before any runs (pre-registration companion to notes/131-ego-selector-experiment).
Results will land in notes/133.

The Headline Result
-------------------
A clean causal demonstration that WHERE the importance function lives matters:
valuation computed against a persistent, self-maintained self-model beats every
cheaper selector — not just at retention, but at adapting what counts as
important. Three contrasts landing together:

### 1. The self-reference effect for machine memory (P2: EGO-STATIC > JUDGE)
The novel core. Both conditions use the same model, same compute, same prompt
length, same counterfactual question ("what if this were forgotten?"); the ONLY
delta is whether the judgment is made relative to a persistent self-model.
Nobody has separated "LLM-as-judge importance scoring" (which Generative
Agents-style systems already do) from "ego-relative valuation." A positive
result is an AI analog of the self-reference effect from cognitive psychology
(Rogers, Kuiper & Kirker 1977) — but operating at the SELECTION layer of
weight-level consolidation, not retrieval. That bridge — consolidation theory
to a falsified/supported functional-ego claim in a running system — is the
publishable novelty.

### 2. The adaptivity boundary (P4: borrowed ego breaks at the goal shift)
The ego paper's Claim 7 concedes that outsourced importance criteria work — the
interesting question is what happens when the frame of concern shifts. Static
rules provably mis-value all five shift-affected facts by construction; a
self-MAINTAINED model re-values within ~2 sleep cycles. An empirical
"borrowed vs resident ego" dissociation does not exist in the literature.

### 3. Surprise is not value, in a deployed architecture (V1 + incumbent characterization)
Our system is TITANS-inspired surprise-gated memory, and the experiment shows
that under decorrelation it selects no better than random — plus the field
note that the production surprise gate had degenerated into a regex
marker-detector with a 0.625 floor (chat.py:250 novelty degeneracy). A
cautionary result about surprise heuristics that ships regardless of the main
outcome.

Why It Is a Paper and Not a Demo
--------------------------------
- Pre-registered predictions with explicit falsification criteria (notes/131 §3)
- Complete floor-to-ceiling control ladder with an ORACLE sensitivity gate
- A corpus where value is provably decorrelated from surprise (blocking checks)
- Full reproducibility on ~$600 of hardware — same reproducibility pitch as the
  VLA plan (notes/130)

Fallback Outcomes (still publishable — this is what the design bought us)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
- P2 holds but P2b fails → the narrowed claim: "any explicit persistent
  valuation frame beats generic importance; ego-specificity shows up only in
  the dynamics." Weaker but honest, and still novel.
- Null on retention with clear separation on selection quality (M3 ρ) →
  "the selector works; 3B consolidation is the bottleneck" — a capacity result
  telling the field ego-like selection only pays above some consolidation
  fidelity.
- Regardless of the matrix: the HallucinationFirewall self-grounding leak is a
  live instance of the paper's Claim 6 ("training on your own reconstructions")
  found in a real system, with the provenance-boundary fix at the curator
  path — workshop-grade on its own.

Scope Honesty
-------------
This experiment cannot claim anything about scale. A 3B result supports
"bounded local agents of this class" (notes/131 §2 scope 1). The 70B question —
where the alignment-tax findings live (notes/62) — remains future work.
